  "final_answer_preview": "'Good faith' is defined in Section 2(11) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, as actions or beliefs that are not considered to be in 'good faith' if they are done or believed without due care and attention. Additionally, Section 31 states that no communication made in good faith is an offence if it is made for the benefit of the person to whom it is directed, even if it causes harm. This indicates that good faith involves a consideration of the intent and the benefit to others, as illustrated in Section 27, where an action taken in good faith for a child's benefit, despite potential harm, is within the exception as long as the intent is not to cause harm.",
